    claim: 'C.OG binary vs probabilistic: price turns before our vol levels more than before placebo levels (P1), and a pre-touch entry (P2 calibrated logistic EV, P3 fixed % of range, P4 sigma-remaining proximity, P5 EV + confirmation) beats exact-touch entry net out of sample',
    result: 'Pre-registered; 25 futures net 2.5 ticks, 645k line approach episodes + placebo, 2.1M pre-touch states; train 2020-22, val 2023, test 2024-2026.08. P1 retreat after coming within 0.10 W: line 15.9% vs placebo 15.8%, +0.14pp [-0.05, +0.32] NULL; episodes touch/retreat/neither 25-30/52-54/16-23% = placebo. P3 B 15%W - A +0.001R, P4 C 0.75sig - A +0.002R, P5 E - D -0.002R (all NULL). P2 D -0.032R [-0.048, -0.017] vs A touch fade -0.184R: gap is cost selection (A1 post-hoc, demoted to NULL); model AUC for trade win 0.505/0.512, Brier = base rate; matched on cost pre-touch states gross ~0. Reach/retreat ARE predictable (logit AUC 0.78/0.73, Brier 0.173 vs 0.219 base); driftless Brownian first passage AUC 0.72-0.81 but under-predicts 6-9pp, drift-carried BM over-predicts 2x; Z (sigma-remaining) distance AUC 0.74 vs ticks 0.59. Brownian bridge mis-calibrated at 1 and 5 min (M1 highs/lows used; 4.4% of misses within 2 ticks). DF: price - open unit root, close - VWAP stationary half-life ~3.5h. Hazards (touch 17.5%/min at Z<0.25, momentum x47) = placebo. Graded weight: against pre-touch entry as an edge; for reach/turn probabilities as context.',
    use: 'Touch-only execution is not leaving money on the table: proximity entries add as many losers as winners and price turns before our levels no more than before placebo levels. Use first-passage / reach probabilities (Z-normalised) as calibrated context, not entries. Only NQ/US30/GOLD/US2000 are cheap enough for 0.25 sigma trades.',
